    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a few complete experts, and *different* tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, serving stacks (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang, DeepSpeed-Inference, Megatron-style runtimes) combine them: dense/attention layers use TP, MoE layers use EP (often with TP *within* an expert, called \"expert-tensor parallelism\"), and data parallelism may sit on top for multi-replica serving.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 What is sharded\n\nFor a transformer layer:\n\n- **Attention**: \n  - QKV projection: column-parallel (each device holds a slice of output heads). With GQA, KV heads are sharded so each device holds the KV heads needed by its query heads.\n  - Output projection `W_O`: row-parallel (each device holds a slice of input features; outputs are *partially summed*).\n- **MLP (dense)**: first GEMM column-parallel, second GEMM row-parallel (Megatron-style). For SwiGLU-style MLPs with three matrices (gate, up, down), gate/up are column-parallel, down is row-parallel.\n- **Embeddings**: either row-parallel (vocab-sharded, requires all-reduce after embedding lookup) or replicated.\n- **LayerNorm /",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
